WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.201 --> 00:00:02.468
-Our next guest
is a talented actor

00:00:02.468 --> 00:00:04.037
you know from his work
on" Shameless."

00:00:04.037 --> 00:00:06.239
He stars in FX's "The Bear,"

00:00:06.239 --> 00:00:08.141
which is streaming
exclusively on Hulu.

00:00:08.141 --> 00:00:09.308
Let's take a look.

00:00:09.308 --> 00:00:10.576
-It's crumbly.
The oven's too dry.

00:00:10.576 --> 00:00:12.211
You need to fill
a baking sheet with water,

00:00:12.211 --> 00:00:14.080
put it on the oven floor,
throw in another batch, okay?

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:15.982
-Don't tell me how to do my job.
-Just do it!

00:00:15.982 --> 00:00:18.818
♪♪

00:00:18.818 --> 00:00:21.854
Yo!
Somebody come try this, cousin!

00:00:21.854 --> 00:00:23.322
-Yeah, right!

00:00:23.322 --> 00:00:25.425
-How you doing, Chef?

00:00:25.425 --> 00:00:29.462
-Chef. What's going on?
What do you think?

00:00:29.462 --> 00:00:31.130
-It's redundant and white,
just like you.

00:00:31.130 --> 00:00:32.432
-Heard, heard, heard.

00:00:32.432 --> 00:00:35.268
-Salt. Heat. It's tender.
-Mmm! Mmm!

00:00:35.268 --> 00:00:37.170
-It's nice? We happy?
-[Bleep]

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:40.473
-Please welcome to the show
Jeremy Allen White, everybody!

00:00:40.473 --> 00:00:42.675
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:42.675 --> 00:00:52.552 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:52.552 --> 00:00:54.821
Welcome to the show!
I'm so happy you're here.

00:00:54.821 --> 00:00:56.722
-I'm so happy to be here.
Thank you for having me.

00:00:56.722 --> 00:00:58.824
-I feel like this is
the show of the summer.

00:00:58.824 --> 00:01:01.894
This is the show
I talk to people about the most.

00:01:01.894 --> 00:01:03.496
Everybody's enjoying it.

00:01:03.496 --> 00:01:06.732
I know you filmed it in Chicago
in the winter, so a bit of time.

00:01:06.732 --> 00:01:07.900
-Yes.
How does it feel, though,

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:09.668
the response?
-Man, it's so nice.

00:01:09.668 --> 00:01:13.105
Like, we were trying
to make a show, I think,

00:01:13.105 --> 00:01:16.408
where, like, back-of-house
and the kitchen industry

00:01:16.408 --> 00:01:17.977
would kind of, like,
give us a head nod,

00:01:17.977 --> 00:01:20.780
and I hope maybe we'd have,
like, a niche audience

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:23.750
that would enjoy it enough
that we could keep doing more.

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:27.086
But I'm incredibly overwhelmed
by how it's really connected

00:01:27.086 --> 00:01:28.921
with so many people,
and it feels good.

00:01:28.921 --> 00:01:31.890
-You play sort of
a renowned chef

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:34.093
who returns to a family business
making sandwiches.

00:01:34.093 --> 00:01:37.429
It is so -- The kitchen scenes
are so chaotic.

00:01:37.429 --> 00:01:38.765
And -- But I feel like

00:01:38.765 --> 00:01:41.267
one of the reasons
people are so energized

00:01:41.267 --> 00:01:43.469
by watching it
is everybody loves eating food.

00:01:43.469 --> 00:01:45.471
And the idea of getting to
watch, like, what actually

00:01:45.471 --> 00:01:47.740
goes through -- even just
to make a beef sandwich.

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:49.542
-Absolutely.
Every restaurant now

00:01:49.542 --> 00:01:51.010
that I look at is a miracle.

00:01:51.010 --> 00:01:55.014
Like, especially in cities
like New York, Chicago, L.A.

00:01:55.014 --> 00:01:59.419
I had no idea
just how much went into it.

00:01:59.419 --> 00:02:02.021
And, yeah, it's incredible.
I have a lot of respect.

00:02:02.021 --> 00:02:04.357
I've spent a lot of time now
with chefs, cooks,

00:02:04.357 --> 00:02:09.195
and the sacrifice of time,
the commitment,

00:02:09.195 --> 00:02:11.831
loss of, like, personal life.

00:02:11.831 --> 00:02:14.734
It's really -- It's astounding,
and it's commendable.

00:02:14.734 --> 00:02:17.170
-I know you did some training
in working kitchens

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:18.771
while you were preparing.
-Oh, yeah.

00:02:18.771 --> 00:02:20.539
-Did the chefs appreciate
you're an actor

00:02:20.539 --> 00:02:23.943
but also hate you a little bit
when you were slow?

00:02:23.943 --> 00:02:26.179
-Yeah, I walked into
every kitchen apologizing

00:02:26.179 --> 00:02:29.348
just like,
"I'm sorry I'm here, too.

00:02:29.348 --> 00:02:32.618
But, like,
please be patient with me."

00:02:32.618 --> 00:02:34.020
And, yeah, it was really nice.

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:35.921
Like, I think everybody
kind of knew the deal, but --

00:02:35.921 --> 00:02:38.424
but there was one time
I was working at this

00:02:38.424 --> 00:02:39.958
restaurant called Pasjoli.

00:02:39.958 --> 00:02:41.127
It's in Santa Monica.

00:02:41.127 --> 00:02:42.828
If you guys
are ever out there, go.

00:02:42.828 --> 00:02:44.063
It's excellent.

00:02:44.063 --> 00:02:45.798
Chef Dave Beran is chef there.

00:02:45.798 --> 00:02:47.567
He was really generous
with his time.

00:02:47.567 --> 00:02:49.001
Everybody kind of knew the deal.

00:02:49.001 --> 00:02:52.038
But one day I was in there,
I was prepping,

00:02:52.038 --> 00:02:54.540
and there was a new line cook,
and I hadn't met her before,

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:56.175
and she was telling me
what to do.

00:02:56.175 --> 00:02:58.177
She told me to do, like,
a three-quarter dice

00:02:58.177 --> 00:02:59.679
on some vegetables.

00:02:59.679 --> 00:03:04.149
I started cutting.
They weren't great.

00:03:04.149 --> 00:03:07.854
And I told her,
"You know what? I'm an actor.

00:03:07.854 --> 00:03:09.355
I'm not a chef."

00:03:09.355 --> 00:03:11.824
And she just goes, "No, I know."

00:03:11.824 --> 00:03:14.761
She could just tell
by how I was cutting.

00:03:14.761 --> 00:03:16.362
Like, there's no -- no way.

00:03:16.362 --> 00:03:18.864
-I like that it was more likely
that an actor

00:03:18.864 --> 00:03:20.233
had come in
to research a role...

00:03:20.233 --> 00:03:23.903
-Than I was actually a chef?
Yeah. Yeah, yeah.

00:03:23.903 --> 00:03:25.438
-And then you went to --

00:03:25.438 --> 00:03:27.272
You took some classes
at the Culinary Institute.

00:03:27.272 --> 00:03:28.941
-Yeah, yeah.
-Do you feel like,

00:03:28.941 --> 00:03:30.643
based on how quickly
you pick things up,

00:03:30.643 --> 00:03:33.212
you would have been
good at this?

00:03:33.212 --> 00:03:34.981
-No. Honestly.
-Thank you for the honesty.

00:03:34.981 --> 00:03:37.516
-I mean, I got it.

00:03:37.516 --> 00:03:39.218
You know, I got really good
at the knife work.

00:03:39.218 --> 00:03:43.422
It's all repetition, really.
It's just time.

00:03:43.422 --> 00:03:46.292
But no. Like, it wasn't --

00:03:46.292 --> 00:03:48.094
It wasn't natural for me,
I'll say, yeah.

00:03:48.094 --> 00:03:51.231
-So, these Italian
beef sandwiches --

00:03:51.231 --> 00:03:53.199
they look so good on the show.
-Yeah.

00:03:53.199 --> 00:03:55.601
-I would imagine
that you had to eat

00:03:55.601 --> 00:03:57.170
your fair share of them.

00:03:57.170 --> 00:03:59.571
-I ate a lot of them on set.
I'm also a big fan.

00:03:59.571 --> 00:04:01.907
I've spent a lot of time
in Chicago over the years,

00:04:01.907 --> 00:04:03.676
and I love the sandwich.

00:04:03.676 --> 00:04:06.378
Like, it is what like the Philly
cheesesteak is for Philly.

00:04:06.378 --> 00:04:10.349
It's really important
to the culture there in Chicago.

00:04:10.349 --> 00:04:12.017
And I ate a lot.

00:04:12.017 --> 00:04:16.022
A lot of hot dogs,
a lot of Italian beefs. Dozens.

00:04:16.022 --> 00:04:18.390
-We have a --
We had an intern run out.

00:04:18.390 --> 00:04:20.827
There's a place in Brooklyn
called Dog Day Afternoon.

00:04:20.827 --> 00:04:23.730
-Oh, whoa.
-And so these are their --

00:04:23.730 --> 00:04:25.698
These are their
Italian beef sandwiches.

00:04:25.698 --> 00:04:27.166
-Oh, wow.
-We've heard good things.

00:04:27.166 --> 00:04:29.202
I mostly just want to -- Here's
what I really want to do.

00:04:29.202 --> 00:04:30.503
'Cause I heard they're good.

00:04:30.503 --> 00:04:32.505
I want to take a bite
of an Italian beef sandwich

00:04:32.505 --> 00:04:34.774
next to you and make the face
that the people on the show

00:04:34.774 --> 00:04:37.143
make every time they eat
one of your sandwiches.

00:04:37.143 --> 00:04:39.311
That's my favorite part
of the show, is --

00:04:39.311 --> 00:04:40.946
-Sure, sure. Here. Cheers.

00:04:40.946 --> 00:04:42.415
-Cheers.
-Cheers. Here we go.

00:04:42.415 --> 00:04:44.149
-This is a Chicago custom.
You cheers your sandwich.

00:04:44.149 --> 00:04:47.053
-That's what you do.

00:04:47.053 --> 00:04:50.389
-Mmm!

00:04:50.389 --> 00:04:53.158
Mmm!
-Mmm.

00:04:53.158 --> 00:04:54.560
And then they never
take another bi--

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:57.863
It's like it's so good that
they're like, "That's fine."

00:04:57.863 --> 00:04:59.465
-"I'm not going to let you
have another bite."

00:04:59.465 --> 00:05:02.001
I'm gonna take them home
with me.

00:05:02.001 --> 00:05:04.336
Um, on the show,

00:05:04.336 --> 00:05:06.071
your character calls
everybody "Chef,"

00:05:06.071 --> 00:05:08.073
and everybody calls you "Chef."

00:05:08.073 --> 00:05:10.309
And you explain it.
It's like the sign of respect

00:05:10.309 --> 00:05:11.811
of everybody in the profession.

00:05:11.811 --> 00:05:15.881
Have people started yelling out
to you, "Hey, Chef," yet?

00:05:15.881 --> 00:05:19.251
-Yes, chef. Yes. They --

00:05:19.251 --> 00:05:21.420
I have this restaurant
I love to go to

00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:23.622
in my neighborhood here in
New York. It's called Rolo's.

00:05:23.622 --> 00:05:26.893
Again, if you guys are in the
neighborhood, go check it out.

00:05:26.893 --> 00:05:30.062
And I go there all the time,
so they know me a little bit.

00:05:30.062 --> 00:05:32.732
But three days after the show
came out, I went there.

00:05:32.732 --> 00:05:36.135
Everybody --
"Chef." "Yes, Chef."

00:05:36.135 --> 00:05:39.338
And it's so -- it's so nice
because like, again,

00:05:39.338 --> 00:05:40.606
kind of what I was
saying before,

00:05:40.606 --> 00:05:42.775
like, we connected, I think,

00:05:42.775 --> 00:05:45.778
with the back-of-house
and the industry in a way

00:05:45.778 --> 00:05:49.482
that I think the show
seems real to them,

00:05:49.482 --> 00:05:54.419
and I don't know
if that's been done yet.

00:05:54.419 --> 00:05:57.189
And so, yeah, I feel --
I feel very lucky to be able

00:05:57.189 --> 00:05:59.091
to walk into a restaurant
and not be booed out,

00:05:59.091 --> 00:06:00.759
but to be called, you know.
"Yes, Chef."

00:06:00.759 --> 00:06:02.428
-I don't know if you've had
this happen.

00:06:02.428 --> 00:06:04.630
I know people that have worked
in restaurants

00:06:04.630 --> 00:06:07.166
and worked in kitchens, and I've
asked them if they watch it.

00:06:07.166 --> 00:06:09.635
And someone said, "Yeah,
but I can't keep watching."

00:06:09.635 --> 00:06:11.770
I'm like, "Oh, is it
not like a restaurant?"

00:06:11.770 --> 00:06:13.472
They're like,
"No, it's way too much..."

00:06:13.472 --> 00:06:15.941
-Too much like a restaurant.
Yes. Yeah, I heard that.

00:06:15.941 --> 00:06:17.910
There's this wonderful article
that came out

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:19.678
in "Bon Appétit,"
which was so cool

00:06:19.678 --> 00:06:21.913
they were even writing
about our show.

00:06:21.913 --> 00:06:25.717
But, yeah, they talked to a lot
of chefs, and, you know,

00:06:25.717 --> 00:06:27.653
a lot of people have a --

00:06:27.653 --> 00:06:30.289
It's -- It's like --
It's a tough environment.

00:06:30.289 --> 00:06:32.024
And that's what we were
trying to show.

00:06:32.024 --> 00:06:34.393
And so, yes,
I think it was a little bit

00:06:34.393 --> 00:06:36.429
traumatizing for --
for some chefs to watch.

00:06:36.429 --> 00:06:39.832
-You know, watching it,
sometimes I was so

00:06:39.832 --> 00:06:42.134
in awe of the choreography

00:06:42.134 --> 00:06:43.802
that must have been required
from all of you,

00:06:43.802 --> 00:06:45.904
because much like a kitchen
at a restaurant,

00:06:45.904 --> 00:06:48.007
especially like that, I mean,
I think most kitchens

00:06:48.007 --> 00:06:50.008
are way tighter
than you expect them to be.

00:06:50.008 --> 00:06:51.778
-Of course. Yeah.
-But just the level

00:06:51.778 --> 00:06:54.713
of the acting, while keeping
in mind that you all had to --

00:06:54.713 --> 00:06:56.149
You know,
you're moving around with --

00:06:56.149 --> 00:06:57.516
Whether they're
prop knives or not.

00:06:57.516 --> 00:06:59.618
Like, it seemed like a dangerous
scene to be doing.

00:06:59.618 --> 00:07:02.555
-Totally. Yeah. I mean,
it was really nice.

00:07:02.555 --> 00:07:04.323
So that was something
I knew I could fake,

00:07:04.323 --> 00:07:07.993
is sort of, like, the movement
through a kitchen,

00:07:07.993 --> 00:07:09.261
what that looks like.

00:07:09.261 --> 00:07:10.929
I was able to, like,
study these really

00:07:10.929 --> 00:07:14.333
wonderful cooks and chefs
and figure out that movement.

00:07:14.333 --> 00:07:16.568
But, yeah, it's a tight spot.

00:07:16.568 --> 00:07:18.537
There's a lot of people,

00:07:18.537 --> 00:07:21.707
and there's a real dance,
like, to the movement,

00:07:21.707 --> 00:07:25.244
but handling knives and pans
and all that stuff,

00:07:25.244 --> 00:07:26.679
that kind of helped me
as an actor

00:07:26.679 --> 00:07:28.681
because you kind of get out
of your own way a little bit.

00:07:28.681 --> 00:07:31.350
You can't -- You can't
be thinking too much

00:07:31.350 --> 00:07:33.185
about what you're saying
or how you're saying it

00:07:33.185 --> 00:07:35.621
because you have, like,
another action.

00:07:35.621 --> 00:07:37.022
Like, I think a prop is like

00:07:37.022 --> 00:07:40.125
a really good friend
to an actor, so...

00:07:40.125 --> 00:07:43.262
So, yeah, I was happy to have
all that stuff to do for sure.

00:07:43.262 --> 00:07:47.199
-You have --
You have fantastic hair,

00:07:47.199 --> 00:07:50.903
and your act-- your acting
in this is wonderful.

00:07:50.903 --> 00:07:55.807
Your hair alone.
Because it's all over the place.

00:07:55.807 --> 00:07:58.978
Like, I feel like the hair --
your hair in a kitchen

00:07:58.978 --> 00:08:00.546
is, like, where
it was meant to be.

00:08:00.546 --> 00:08:03.148
-For sure. For sure.
I mean, I agree.

00:08:03.148 --> 00:08:06.451
I wanted -- I saw --
I read this book,

00:08:06.451 --> 00:08:09.288
Marco Pierre White's book,
and there's this photo of him

00:08:09.288 --> 00:08:11.323
on the front of the book,
and he's got all this long hair.

00:08:11.323 --> 00:08:13.058
And so I was like, I really
want to grow my hair out

00:08:13.058 --> 00:08:15.328
and kind of have a little nod
to Marco Pierre White.

00:08:15.328 --> 00:08:17.162
And so I did it.

00:08:17.162 --> 00:08:20.132
I thought it looked great
on the show, but it's weird.

00:08:20.132 --> 00:08:22.801
A lot of people are calling
out -- and they're not wrong --

00:08:22.801 --> 00:08:24.203
that I should have been
wearing a cap.

00:08:24.203 --> 00:08:25.738
I should have been wearing,
like, something

00:08:25.738 --> 00:08:27.706
because, like,
so much of Carmy's hair

00:08:27.706 --> 00:08:29.608
is probably
in all those sandwiches.

00:08:29.608 --> 00:08:31.577
-Yeah, that's a fair part.
That's a fair point.

00:08:31.577 --> 00:08:34.213
But yet every time people take
a bite, they don't seem to mind.

00:08:34.213 --> 00:08:36.415
-Right.
And how I can't put a hat on.

00:08:36.415 --> 00:08:39.985
-Yeah.
It always looks like the hair

00:08:39.985 --> 00:08:42.221
that if you needed something
from somebody and you asked them

00:08:42.221 --> 00:08:43.722
and they had that hair,
you'd be like,

00:08:43.722 --> 00:08:45.424
"Oh, you're not right.
You can't handle this."

00:08:45.424 --> 00:08:47.025
-Sure. Yes.
-You have the hair of somebody

00:08:47.025 --> 00:08:50.295
who's one task past
what they can deal with.

00:08:50.295 --> 00:08:53.132
-Totally. Yes. The entirety
of the show is -- Yes.

00:08:53.132 --> 00:08:55.701
-You had some professional
chefs, including Matty Matheson,

00:08:55.701 --> 00:08:57.403
who's been a guest on this show

00:08:57.403 --> 00:08:58.638
who's an incredibly
charismatic guy

00:08:58.638 --> 00:08:59.905
who's an actor
on the show, as well.

00:08:59.905 --> 00:09:02.141
-Yeah.
[ Applause ]

00:09:02.141 --> 00:09:05.378
-So Matty, who was,

00:09:05.378 --> 00:09:07.913
you know, incredibly funny
and charismatic when he's here.

00:09:07.913 --> 00:09:09.749
Wonderful on the show.
-Yeah.

00:09:09.749 --> 00:09:11.983
-And also was sort of
a technical advisor.

00:09:11.983 --> 00:09:13.585
-Totally. 100%.

00:09:13.585 --> 00:09:16.589
-And I know, also, the show's
creator -- his sister.

00:09:16.589 --> 00:09:18.224
-Chris Storer's sister,
Courtney Storer,

00:09:18.224 --> 00:09:20.126
is a wonderful chef
at a restaurant.

00:09:20.126 --> 00:09:21.960
Or she used to be
at a restaurant.

00:09:21.960 --> 00:09:23.762
Jon &amp; and Vinny's
in Los Angeles.

00:09:23.762 --> 00:09:25.363
She's really talented.

00:09:25.363 --> 00:09:28.367
And her and Matty were in charge
of a lot of stuff.

00:09:28.367 --> 00:09:31.203
But for me personally,
I told them,

00:09:31.203 --> 00:09:33.205
"If you see me
doing something goofy,

00:09:33.205 --> 00:09:34.740
if you see me doing something

00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:36.442
that does not make sense
in the kitchen,

00:09:36.442 --> 00:09:38.711
please yell, 'Cut.' Stop me."

00:09:38.711 --> 00:09:40.812
And Matty would, all the time.

00:09:40.812 --> 00:09:42.714
-Matty does not strike me

00:09:42.714 --> 00:09:44.149
as a guy
who would have any hesitation.

00:09:44.149 --> 00:09:45.985
-No, no, no.
-Well, that's good.

00:09:45.985 --> 00:09:48.420
-But he's excellent.
I mean, like, the show --

00:09:48.420 --> 00:09:50.489
The show looks the way it does
and feels the way it does

00:09:50.489 --> 00:09:53.792
because of of Matty
and Courtney.

00:09:53.792 --> 00:09:56.461
Like, they're real chefs,
and they're excellent.

00:09:56.461 --> 00:09:59.131
-Well,
it's a really special show.

00:09:59.131 --> 00:10:00.966
Congratulations to you
and everybody who worked on it.

00:10:00.966 --> 00:10:02.535
And thanks so much
for being here.

00:10:02.535 --> 00:10:05.204
I really can't thank you enough.
Jeremy Allen White, everybody.

00:10:05.204 --> 00:10:07.339
FX's "The Bear" is
streaming exclusively on Hulu.

00:10:07.339 --> 00:10:09.408
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

